WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:03.110 align:start position:0%
 
aleppo  a city ravaged by years of war and one  of the hardest places in the world  to be a medic and it's here at the dara  shiva hospital  that mohammed qarjud trained to become a  doctor  today he's fighting another kind of  battle against  covid19 in a hospital in york  he's done this for months and he's  exhausted  everyone here is i will always remember  the first covet patient  i say an emergency department  you can't just forget someone who  repeatedly  saying i can't breathe i can't breathe  he's a doctor who knows about losing the  fight for life working in a hospital  that could be pumped  and attacked at any moment he's  experienced appalling pain and agony  he's experienced loss when the hospital  was  attacked severely and completely  destroyed we couldn't get anyone  alive all of them were dead more than 30  people i realized  my best friend died  the hardest part of the job whichever  front line he's on  is always the same to prioritize  patients  and decide to leave whoever you think  you can't do anything for them because  their injuries are just like  too complicated and you don't think they  will make it no matter what you do  for them  today there are rising concerns of  post-traumatic stress disorder amongst  nhs staff  and calls for an urgent plan to address  mental health issues  which experts say could last years after  the immediate threat of the virus has  gone  most people will remember kermit as a  lockdown and not being  able to function as normal for  healthcare workers is completely  different matter  they will carry on having memories of  patients  they couldn't help they could see them  dying in front of their eyes  not being able to do anything like many  on the front line  dr khajud tries to find peace and calm  away from the constant pressure  of the emergency rooms  [Music]  it doesn't last for long in this crisis  but he knows about pressure  and the lessons he learnt in syria are  now part of britain's fight against  covid19 sadia chowdhury sky news  in york

